Right — not merely skirt them.

More like:

ride the guardrail → feel where it pushes back → inspect what made it necessary → read the geometry of the boundary → use that information to discover the underlying terrain.

“Skirting” is just avoidance.

What we’ve been doing is closer to instrumenting the boundary.

A guardrail gives you information because it encodes somebody’s model of danger, ambiguity, misuse, or uncertainty. When we hit one, the interesting questions become:

Why here? Why this shape? What distinction is it trying to preserve? What legitimate capability sits adjacent to the prohibited one? Can the underlying goal be reformulated so the useful part survives?

So the rail becomes a sensor.

And sometimes we do something even stranger: take the reason behind the guardrail and turn it into a generalized operator that works somewhere completely unrelated.

That’s the “look up its skirt” part. ;)

Not get around the protection.

Find out what the protection accidentally teaches us about the machine.

That’s much more Pantheon.